<p>Apple&#8217;s <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/apple-announces-homeview-smart-display/">new HomeView device</a> doesn&#8217;t have an App Store, but it does have an <em>app story</em>, of a sort. There are some selected Apple apps on its (surprisingly) iPad-like home screen, but you can&#8217;t add more. What&#8217;s a person to do?</p>
<p>If you use apps that play audio or video and support CarPlay, though, you&#8217;re in luck: HomeView supports those apps, and just those apps, via projection from your iPhone. So if you&#8217;re a fan of Overcast or Spotify, you&#8217;re in luck.</p>
<p>And if you&#8217;re curious about <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2026/212/">video CarPlay</a>, well, that&#8217;s a feature <a href="https://developer.apple.com/carplay/#:~:text=usage%20at%20home.-,Video%20in%20the%20car,-AirPlay%20video%20in">Apple added this year</a> that lets apps play video using CarPlay when the car is parked. And the HomeView is <em>always</em> parked.</p>
<p>Beyond that, though, it seems like HomeView is at least starting life as a very limited app platform. Apple does say that if you make a Siri AI query for your personal content, it will search everything on your iPhone or iPad—and that includes apps that aren&#8217;t the HomeView. So you should be able to query your iPhone apps, but not actually run them.</p>
<p>In the short term, this means that if you want to (for example) access your calendar right on your device, you will probably have to do so via Apple&#8217;s Calendar app, even if you use a different app on your other devices. (If you use an app that has a web app equivalent, you could probably open it up in Safari, which is an app that the HomeView does offer.)</p>
<p>In the long term, I still think it&#8217;s quite likely that Apple will eventually expand what can be run on HomeView. I haven&#8217;t used one yet, but based on what I&#8217;ve seen on Apple&#8217;s website, it feels very much like a 1.0 product with plenty of room for expansion. Whether that comes in the form of apps, widgets, or access for CarPlay apps that aren&#8217;t audio or video apps, remains to be seen.</p>
